# Copy to .env. Never commit the result.

DATABASE_URL=postgres://klopt:klopt@localhost:5432/klopt

# S3-compatible storage. The documents bucket must have object lock enabled:
# it holds statutory records under a 7-year retention obligation, and the lock
# is what makes that the storage's promise rather than the application's.
#
# Leave KLOPT_S3_ENDPOINT unset and documents go in a directory instead — the
# default in a fresh install, per spec 8's first rule. Setting it *without* the
# bucket and credentials is refused at boot rather than falling back, because a
# silent fallback would put statutory records somewhere nobody meant.
#
# `mc mb --with-lock` is what enables object lock, and it can only be done when
# the bucket is created. `--ignore-existing` will happily leave an existing
# bucket without one, so a bucket made before this was set up has to be
# recreated.
#
# This is for running the application, not for running the tests. The S3 store's
# specs bring up their own object-lock bucket in-process; exporting this in the
# shell that runs them points them at the real bucket instead, which is a
# deliberate opt-in rather than a requirement (ADR 0059).
KLOPT_S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
KLOPT_S3_REGION=us-east-1
KLOPT_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=klopt
KLOPT_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=klopt-dev-secret
KLOPT_S3_DOCUMENTS_BUCKET=klopt-documents
# `compliance` cannot be bypassed by anybody, including the account root, and is
# what the bewaarplicht wants. `governance` can be bypassed by a caller holding
# s3:BypassGovernanceRetention, and exists for a bucket somebody inherited.
KLOPT_S3_OBJECT_LOCK_MODE=compliance

# Encrypts adapter credentials at rest — a mailbox password, an API token, an
# Exact Online client secret and its refresh token.
# Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# At least 32 bytes of key material; shorter values are refused (audit M6).
# In production this comes from a KMS, never from a file.
#
# With none set, a credential is refused rather than stored as typed: the
# alternative is a password somebody else finds in a dump. Nothing in a default
# install needs one — the drop directory that documents arrive in has nothing to
# authenticate to. Connecting to Exact Online does: without a key the
# connection is refused rather than stored, and the screen says so before
# anybody pastes a secret in.
KLOPT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Signs session cookies. Generate the same way. Changing it signs everyone out.
# Required for the web UI; a headless deployment that only serves API tokens
# does not need it.
KLOPT_AUTH_SECRET=

# Where this instance is reached. Sign-in links and invitations point at it, and
# an `https://` value is what makes the session cookie `secure`.
#
# It has to be the origin the browser actually uses. Setting it to https while
# browsing http://localhost:3000 breaks sign-in — the cookie is issued `secure`
# and the plain-http page cannot send it back.
#
# In production (`NODE_ENV=production`) the process refuses to boot unless this
# is an `https://` origin — otherwise better-auth would issue a non-Secure
# session cookie (audit M4).
#
# `pnpm dev` serves http://localhost:3000. `pnpm dev:https` serves
# https://klopt.localhost through portless, which is what Exact Online's
# redirect URI needs — see the Exact block below.
KLOPT_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# After the firm is on the instance, set this to `closed` so a stranger who can
# receive mail at a new address cannot provision their own books. Invite-by-
# address on Toegang still adds people to an existing administration (audit M3).
# KLOPT_SIGNUP=closed

# Compliance artefacts (RGS, XAF schema). Defaults to ./reference-data.
KLOPT_REFERENCE_DATA_DIR=

# Email. Sign-in codes, and from M1 invoices and dunning.
#
# With none of this set, messages are written to the log instead of being sent,
# so a fresh install still works and you can read your own sign-in code out of
# the container output. Set KLOPT_EMAIL_OUTBOX_DIR to write them to files
# instead, which is easier to read while setting an instance up.
#
# Production refuses the log transport: configure SMTP or KLOPT_EMAIL_OUTBOX_DIR
# (audit M2).
KLOPT_SMTP_HOST=
KLOPT_SMTP_PORT=587
KLOPT_SMTP_USER=
KLOPT_SMTP_PASSWORD=
KLOPT_SMTP_FROM="Klopt <klopt@example.com>"
# KLOPT_SMTP_SECURE defaults to true on port 465, false otherwise.
KLOPT_SMTP_SECURE=
# KLOPT_EMAIL_OUTBOX_DIR=./.outbox

# Allow webhook delivery and IMAP hosts that resolve to private / link-local /
# loopback addresses. For local compose networks and laptop mail servers only.
# Do not set this on an internet-facing instance (audit M1, L4).
# KLOPT_ALLOW_PRIVATE_OUTBOUND=1

# Where source documents are kept. Content-addressed; defaults to
# ./.klopt/documents. The worker needs the same path as the web app.
KLOPT_DOCUMENT_DIR=

# A timestamp authority to witness sealed snapshots (RFC 3161, ADR 0058).
#
# Unset by default, and a seal is worth having without one — it still detects a
# change. What it cannot do is prove its own date to somebody who does not take
# your word for it, which is the situation a sealed snapshot exists for. Point
# this at any RFC 3161 endpoint and every seal taken from then on carries the
# authority's signed "I saw this hash at this time".
#
# Only the seal is sent: 32 bytes over a manifest that is itself a few
# kilobytes. Nothing about the administration leaves the building.
#
#   KLOPT_TIMESTAMP_URL=https://freetsa.org/tsr
#
# Some authorities require a policy to be named. Most do not.
KLOPT_TIMESTAMP_URL=
KLOPT_TIMESTAMP_POLICY_OID=

KLOPT_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4

# Exact Online has no environment variables. The OAuth app belongs to whoever
# runs this instance, so its client id and secret are registered per
# administration on the Exact Online screen and encrypted with the key above —
# spec 8's bring-your-own-credential rule.
#
# What to register at https://apps.exactonline.com (App Center → My apps):
#
#   Redirect URI   <KLOPT_BASE_URL>/exact/callback
#                  e.g. https://klopt.localhost/exact/callback
#
# Exact compares the redirect URI literally, so the scheme, host, port and path
# all have to match what the screen sends. That is why the field is filled in
# rather than derived: guessing it right most of the time is worse than asking.
#
# ## The redirect has to be https, which localhost is not by default
#
# Exact will not accept a plain-http redirect URI. So local development goes
# through portless, which fronts the dev server with a locally-trusted HTTPS
# certificate on a stable .localhost name:
#
#   npm install -g portless        # once, globally
#   portless proxy start           # once; asks for sudo to bind 443
#   pnpm dev:https                 # -> https://klopt.localhost
#
# Then set KLOPT_BASE_URL=https://klopt.localhost and register
# https://klopt.localhost/exact/callback at Exact.
#
# `portless service install` starts the proxy at boot, so the sudo prompt
# happens once rather than every morning.
#
# The name is pinned to `klopt` in the script rather than left to portless to
# infer. A bare `portless` discovers the workspace and prefixes the git branch
# onto the host — `klopt-requirements.klopt.localhost` — so the URL would move
# whenever the branch did, and a literally-compared redirect URI cannot follow
# it. The cost is that two worktrees cannot serve on that name at once; run one
# of them with `portless <other-name> pnpm exec vite dev` and register its
# callback too.
#
# The app needs no scopes to be chosen — Exact grants what the signed-in user
# can already reach, which is every administration they have rights to. That is
# exactly why choosing the division is its own step (ADR 0034).
